<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Amos Comenius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1592">1592</a>–<a href="/years/1670">1670</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jan Amos Komenský, Comenius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Amos Comenius was a Czech philosopher, educator and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the Unity of the Brethren. Driven into exile after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a>, he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. His books, among them the illustrated &quot;Orbis Pictus&quot; and the &quot;Great Didactic&quot;, argued for universal education and made him a founder of modern teaching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius.jpg" alt="John Amos Comenius" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jürgen Ovens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Amos_Comenius_(Komensky)_(1592-1670)._Tsjechisch_humanist_en_pedagoog._Als_voorganger_van_de_Moravische_of_Boheemse_Broedergemeente_verdreven_en_sedert_1656_gevestigd_te_Amsterdam_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-2161.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Unity of the Brethren, which grew out of it, produced the Bible of Kralice and the educator <b>John Amos Comenius</b>, and its exiles later revived as the Moravian Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brethren printed the six-volume Kralice Bible between 1579 and 1593, and <b>Jan Amos Comenius</b>, born in Moravia in 1592, became their best-known bishop.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uherský Brod, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.03,17.65&amp;year=1642" aria-label="Uherský Brod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4958 453 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Amos Comenius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5408 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5408 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.03,17.65&amp;year=1642">What was here in 1642? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I say, and say it again aloud, that this last is the foundation of all the rest: because we can neither act nor speak wisely, unless we first rightly understand all the things which are to be done, and whereof we are to speak.&#8221;</p><footer>Orbis Pictus (1658), preface &#183; tr. Charles Hoole, 1659 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28299/pg28299-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
